      the console and about that published material, and about nothing else: it does not say that everything
      OpenFray may ever offer is free of charge, and anything that is not will say so plainly where it
      is offered.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Accounts, and agreeing to these terms</h2>
    <p>
      You can use OpenFray anonymously, or sign in with a Google or Discord account to save your
      work. There is no password to set: we never see one, and access to your account is access to
      the Google or Discord account behind it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Creating an account means accepting these terms.</strong> The sign-in screen asks you to
      confirm it before it will hand you to Google or Discord, and it links this page so you can read
      it first. Using the Service without an account is acceptance too, on the same terms, minus every
      section that only applies to an account.
    </p>
    <p>
      You are responsible for what happens under your account and for keeping access to the identity
      provider you signed in with. Losing that account loses this one: there is no other way to
      prove an account is yours, and we cannot restore one.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Minimum age</h2>
    <p>
      You must be at least <strong>13 years old</strong> to use the Service, and at least the minimum
      digital-consent age in your country where that is higher (16 in some of the EU). If you are under
      that age, do not create an account or publish anything. If we learn that an account belongs to somebody
      under it, we will close the account and delete what is on it.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Acceptable use</h2>
    <p>You agree not to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>use the Service to break the law or infringe others’ rights;</li>
