      Content, or your whole account, at any time from within the app.
    </p>

    <h2>7. What you publish</h2>
    <p>
      The Service lets you publish an encounter, or a single creature, to a link anyone can open.
      <strong>Publishing requires an account.</strong> Using the console does not, and that has not changed;
      publishing puts something of yours on a public address, and we ask you to sign in first so that
      a published page has somebody standing behind it and so that you can take it down again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Publishing is a choice you make one encounter or one creature at a time. What travels is the
      cast or the stat block, any note you write with it, and any byline you type; it never includes
      your player characters, hit points, or anything else about a fight in progress. For that
      content, and only for what you publish, you grant us the license needed to host it, display it
      publicly, and let others copy it onto their own board.
    </p>
    <p>
      Publishing has limits: a single encounter or creature has to fit a modest size, so a very large
      or heavily homebrewed cast may need trimming before it publishes, and an account can only hold
      so many published pages at once — take one down to publish another once you reach it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>You confirm you hold the rights to what you publish.</strong> A creature you wrote is yours
      to share; one you copied out of a book you bought is not, and a stat block someone else published
      is theirs on whatever terms they gave it. The console records what each creature says about itself
      and shows it on the page, but it cannot check whether a claim is true, and publishing is you stating
      that it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What anyone opening the link may do with it</strong>: run it at their table, and,
      where the license on a creature permits reuse, add that creature to their own library in the
      app. That is the point of a shared link, and it needs no further permission from you. Anything
      beyond it — republishing a creature, putting it in something of their own — is governed by the
      license each creature carries, not by the fact that they were able to open the link.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>You may state how what you publish can be reused.</strong> An encounter carries a license
      covering your own words — its name, your note, and the arrangement of the cast — and each creature
      carries whatever license its author gave it. Stating none is the default, and it is not a grant:
