      who did not hold the rights to it. If what you are reporting is your own copyright, section 8
      says what to send.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If we remove a page you published, we tell you</strong>, at the address on your
      account, and say that it was reported and found in breach of these terms. Removal is final:
      the page is deleted, and the link answers nothing afterwards. We may also stop an account from
      publishing, without closing it, where what it publishes keeps breaking these terms.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Copyright complaints</h2>
    <p>
      If something published through the Service infringes a copyright you hold, write to <a
        href="mailto:reports@openfray.app">reports@openfray.app</a
      > and tell us:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the link to the page, so we can find exactly what you mean;</li>
      <li>what on it is yours, and where the original can be seen;</li>
      <li>who you are, and how to reach you;</li>
      <li>
        that you hold that right or act for whoever does, and that you believe in good faith the use
        is not authorized.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We read these ourselves, and we act on the ones that hold up. A page we remove for this reason
      is deleted, and the person who published it is told what was removed and why, at the address
      on their account.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If your page was removed and you believe that was wrong</strong>, reply to that
      message and say so. Tell us why you hold the rights, and we will look again. We would rather
      restore something we took down in error than leave it down because nobody argued.
    </p>
    <p>Repeated infringement costs an account its ability to publish, and can close it.</p>

